---
import LabLayout from '../../layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const sections = [
  { id: 'objective', label: 'Objective' },
  { id: 'capture', label: 'Capture' },
  { id: 'observations', label: 'Observations' },
  { id: 'analysis', label: 'Analysis' },
  { id: 'next', label: 'What I would add' },
  { id: 'lesson', label: 'Lesson' },
];
---

<LabLayout
  title="Wireshark: reading a path through ICMP"
  subtitle="Localhost, the gateway, and the internet. Same protocol, three different paths."
  kicker="Packet analysis / Wireshark"
  description="A packet-capture exercise comparing ICMP echo requests and replies to localhost, a LAN gateway, and a public host."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Environment', value: 'Wireshark · local host' },
    { label: 'Destinations', value: '127.0.0.1 · 10.0.2.1 · google.com' },
    { label: 'Tools', value: 'Wireshark · ping' },
    { label: 'Date', value: '2026-07-11' },
  ]}
  sections={sections}
  repoUrl={`${SITE.githubUrl}/5-wireshark`}
  outcomes={[
    { value: '3', label: 'destinations' },
    { value: '4', label: 'requests each' },
    { value: '0', label: 'failed replies' },
    { value: '1', label: 'capture file' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="objective" class="article-section">
    <h2>Objective</h2>
    <p>
      Capture ICMP echo requests and replies to three destinations and compare the
      round-trip time. The point was to practice capture filters, timestamps, and reading a
      path — not to discover a new protocol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="capture" class="article-section">
    <h2>Capture</h2>
    <p>
      I sent four echo requests to each destination and captured the traffic in Wireshark.
      The filter below keeps echo requests and replies:
    </p>
    <div class="article-code">
      icmp.type == 8 || icmp.type == 0
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li><code>127.0.0.1</code> — the local stack, no network path.</li>
      <li><code>10.0.2.1</code> — the VirtualBox gateway on the local network.</li>
      <li><code>google.com</code> — a public host reached through the internet.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="observations" class="article-section">
    <h2>Observations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each destination returned four echo requests and four echo replies.</li>
      <li>Localhost was the fastest: the packets never left the machine.</li>
      <li>The gateway was fast: one LAN hop, no internet path.</li>
      <li>Google was the slowest: more hops, more queues, more variability.</li>
      <li>No requests failed.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="analysis" class="article-section">
    <h2>Analysis</h2>
    <p>
      ICMP is a simple probe, but it makes path length visible. The localhost path exercises
      the kernel’s loopback path. The gateway path exercises the virtual network and the
      local link. The public path adds routing, peering, and the destination’s response
      policy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The comparison is a baseline, not a latency benchmark. ICMP can be deprioritized, and
      four packets are not enough to characterize jitter. A useful next step would be to
      repeat the capture under load and compare with TCP handshake timing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="next" class="article-section">
    <h2>What I would add</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Timestamp deltas and a small table of min/avg/max RTT per destination.</li>
      <li>A traceroute capture to correlate hops with latency.</li>
      <li>TCP handshake timing for a more realistic application path.</li>
      <li>Repeated runs to separate noise from a stable difference.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lesson" class="article-section">
    <h2>Lesson</h2>
    <p>
      A packet capture is only useful if you know what question you are asking. Here the
      question was “where does the time go?” The answer was visible in the path, not in a
      single number.
    </p>
  </section>
</LabLayout>
